#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133492 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e46fa568-0a40-5b36-a01f-20555db9b055" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inner Product of Two Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "d8ab385f-ca7e-5ac2-9c0c-23ec78c9b1f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-5542" :question/problem "An inner product on $V = \\mathbb{R}^3$ is given by $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle = 3 x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + 7 x_3 y_3$. Find $\\langle \\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\rangle$, if\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the inner product according to the definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x, y⟩ &= 3x_{1}y_{1} + x_{2}y_{2} + 7x_{3}y_{3} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-3) + (-2) \\cdot 1 + 7 \\cdot 0 \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&=-9 - 2 + 0 \\\\\n&=-11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133493 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efed2bff-625e-556d-99a3-9b51f9d68937" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inner Product of Two Vectors in a Vector Space of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "d7217dd1-5e4d-5ecb-96a4-e4c7064f2f88" :question/math-academy-id "e-5543" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathbb{R}_2[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product\n$\\langle {x}(t), {y}(t) \\rangle = x(1)y(1) + x(2)y(2) + x(3)y(3)$.\nFind $\\langle \\mathbf{x}(t), \\mathbf{y}(t) \\rangle$, if\n${x}(t) = 1-2t+3t^2, \\quad {y}(t) = t-t^2$." :question/worked-solution "Computing the inner product according to the definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n⟨x(t), y(t)⟩ &= x(1)y(1) + x(2)y(2) + x(3)y(3) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 0 + 9 \\cdot (-2) + 22 \\cdot (-6) \\\\\n&= 0 - 18 - 132 \\\\\n&=-150\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133494 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c553cb8-9f5c-5fa5-bc43-db7d7b64755f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inner Product of Two Vectors in a Vector Space of Continuous Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "007b171e-6587-5835-a59c-443b29c2b667" :question/math-academy-id "e-5544" :question/problem "Let $V=\\mathcal{C}\\left[0,2\\pi\\right]$, the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $\\left[0,2\\pi\\right]$. Find $\\langle \\sin t, \\cos t \\rangle$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n$\\langle x(t),y(t) \\rangle = \\displaystyle\\int_{0}^{2\\pi} x(t) y(t) \\, \\textrm{d}t$." :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\langle \\sin t, \\cos t \\rangle = \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}\\sin{t} \\cos{t} \\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity\n\n$$\n\\sin2t =2\\sin t\\cos t\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{2π}\\sin t\\cos tdt &= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{2π}\\sin 2tdt \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4}\\cos 2t \\mid _{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4}(\\cos 4π - \\cos 0) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{4}(1 - 1) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e46fa568-0a40-5b36-a01f-20555db9b055" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inner Product of Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97694f59-7fe3-5365-a1cf-785a14149844" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132279} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a64bc4-b71b-5a87-a7d2-a08c49f0fdaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132280} {:answer/id #uuid "e80d8c55-f19e-5464-825d-2df656ce62a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132281} {:answer/id #uuid "2582659f-fad9-52a0-a50c-7eefc9224ac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132282} {:answer/id #uuid "5012543b-11ef-5095-8361-dcf6950cdf4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7a64bc4-b71b-5a87-a7d2-a08